   "node": "S5.t2_t14",
   "issue": "The 'Step 2' trap (shrink to 2 cm, then conserve) is refuted only as a 'loophole around a fixed size rule'; it never says why step 2 is wrong, so the refutation leaves a careful learner unsure. The correct answer is also a negative claim ('which step is wrong')."
  },
  {
   "node": "S7.p5",
   "issue": "Negative stems ('which is NOT correct', 'which is NOT part of the plan') put the true answer on the false statement; S5.t4_nerve does the same. Easy to misread on a fast tap."
  },
  {
   "node": "S7.p1",
   "issue": "Segment 7 is a 14-probe drill of the same shape (question, trap, partial, correct, Next). For a learner who already fixed the beliefs it is dead weight, about 40 taps re-asking refuted traps with the same options."
  },
  {
   "node": "S2.entry_t10",
   "issue": "The sure note says 'a common type is not the answer to a question about spread', but the question asked about the type of tissue pattern, not spread."
  },
  {
   "node": "S4.t_n_reveal",
   "issue": "Three ideas in one message: AJCC counts contralateral nodes as M1, older PYQs expected M1 for any supraclavicular node, and same-side nodes are N3c. Lost the thread on the first read."
  },
  {
